   "read_on": "2026-10-26",
   "how_read": "Public Pew report PDFs, toplines and chart images only, PDFs converted with pdftotext -layout and chart PNGs read by eye. Totals are Pew's printed NET (a great deal plus a fair amount, computed by Pew from unrounded data), from the release named in each wave's source_url; the topline (https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2026/01/PS_2026.1.15-trust-in-science_topline.pdf) carries every wave's four answers, and the NET differs from the sum of the rounded parts in Dec 2018, Dec 2021 and Oct 2024 (noted per wave). Cells: the 2026 report's appendix table prints the White, Black and Hispanic NET for all 11 waves; the 2020 report and the 2016 short read also print sex and education groups, kept where they are one frame category. Pew defines White and Black adults as single-race, non-Hispanic and Hispanic adults as of any race, the frame's own coding (HISP first, then RAC1P). 'College grad' / 'College degree' is a bachelor's degree with postgraduate shown separately, and Pew's 'Some college' includes associate degrees, as the frame's some_college does. Not kept: Asian (inside the frame's other), party, the education unions 'College grad+', 'Some college or less' and 'HS or less', generations and science-knowledge groups. 'published' is the first release found that prints the wave's total. Race NETs appear in the wave's own release for Apr 2020, Oct 2023, Oct 2024 and Oct 2025; for the other seven waves the earliest source found is the 2026 appendix (2026-01-15), so a strict out-of-time cell backtest should treat those cells as published 2026-01-15."
  }
 },
